@page "/tables/advanced-table/sorting"

@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button.Enums
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.AdvancedTable.Components
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.AdvancedTable.Components.Columns

<h3>Column-level sort</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Add <code>Sortable</code> to any <code>AdvancedTableColumn</code> to make it user-sortable.
    Click once for ascending, again for descending.
</DescriptionBanner>

<AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Sortable="true">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Sortable="true">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp">
            <CellContent>@context.Timestamp</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>

<h3>Sort by more than one column</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <kbd>Shift</kbd>-click a header to add its column to the sort instead of replacing it. The added column becomes the
    lowest priority, so it decides the order of the rows that tie on everything sorted before it. <kbd>Shift</kbd>-clicking
    a column that is already sorted flips its direction and keeps its priority, while a plain click drops
    every column but the clicked one.

    Every row below ties with two others on <code>Key</code>. Sort by <code>Key</code>, then <kbd>Shift</kbd>-click
    <code>Value</code> to decide the order inside each group. Applying every entry is the items provider's job —
    the one behind this page chains them, a provider that reads only the first entry would ignore the rest.
</DescriptionBanner>

<AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_tiedItemsProvider" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>

<h3>Start with a sort — an unchanging <code>SortingState</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Pass <code>SortingState</code> from a field that is never reassigned to give the table the sort it starts
    with. The table applies it once the columns have registered and owns it from then on, because handing over
    the same instance again is not a new command — clicking a header changes the sort and nothing puts it back.
    An entry for a column the table does not show is dropped. A plain header click replaces the whole value, so
    a sort over several columns survives a stray click only as far as <kbd>Shift</kbd>-clicking rebuilds it.
</DescriptionBanner>

<AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" SortingState="_seedSortingState" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Timestamp</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>

<h3>Observe changes — <code>SortingStateChanged</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Handle <code>SortingStateChanged</code> without passing <code>SortingState</code> to watch the applied sort
    without driving it. It reports the applied sorting after every change, whatever caused it — a header click,
    a change made from outside, or a column disappearing. The list below reports what was last raised.
</DescriptionBanner>

@if (_observedSortingState.ColumnSortings.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var columnSorting in _observedSortingState.ColumnSortings)
        {
            <li><code>@columnSorting.ColumnId</code> — @(columnSorting.Ascending ? "ascending" : "descending")</li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>Nothing observed yet — click a column header.</em></p>
}

<AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" SortingStateChanged="ObservedSortingStateChanged" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Timestamp</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>

<h3>Drive the sort from markup — <code>@("@bind-SortingState")</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Bind <code>SortingState</code> to drive the table from a field and have every change written back into it,
    whoever made it — the buttons below or a column header. A new instance in the field is a command; the same
    instance again is not, which is what lets the table's own value flow back without starting over.

    <strong>Note:</strong> build the value in a field and only replace it when it really changes. A parent that
    produces a fresh, content-equal instance on every render makes the table re-apply on every render, and under
    a binding that never terminates — each apply reports back, each report renders the parent, each render
    builds another instance.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="options">
    <div class="option">
        <label>Sort order</label>
        <Button Text="Sort by Key" OnClick="SortBoundTableByKey" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
    <div class="option">
        <label>Sort order</label>
        <Button Text="Clear sort" OnClick="ClearBoundSorting" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
</div>

@if (_boundSortingState.ColumnSortings.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var columnSorting in _boundSortingState.ColumnSortings)
        {
            <li><code>@columnSorting.ColumnId</code> — @(columnSorting.Ascending ? "ascending" : "descending")</li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>No active sort.</em></p>
}

<AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider"
               RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
               @bind-SortingState="_boundSortingState"
               MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" Sortable="true" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Timestamp</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>

<h3>Change the sort from outside — <code>SetSortingStateAsync</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Hold the table in an <code>@("@ref")</code> and call <code>SetSortingStateAsync</code> for the changes a
    binding expresses badly — a reset, a jump to a preset — and for callers that are not in a render at all,
    such as a debounce timer or a server push. Unlike the parameter the method has no memory of what it was
    handed before, so resetting works here even though the empty sorting is a value the table has seen before.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="options">
    <div class="option">
        <label>Sort order</label>
        <Button Text="Reset sort" OnClick="ResetSortingAsync" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
</div>

@if (_pushedSortingState.ColumnSortings.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var columnSorting in _pushedSortingState.ColumnSortings)
        {
            <li><code>@columnSorting.ColumnId</code> — @(columnSorting.Ascending ? "ascending" : "descending")</li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>No active sort.</em></p>
}

<AdvancedTable @ref="_pushTargetTable"
               ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider"
               RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
               SortingStateChanged="PushedSortingStateChanged"
               MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Sortable="true">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Sortable="true">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" Sortable="true">
            <CellContent>@context.Timestamp</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>
